  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that estradiol may interact with Cathepsin E through multiple pathways, with some targets acting as intermediaries. Estradiol is linked to Sulfotransferase family cytosolic 2B member 1, which in turn targets P-Nitrophenol, and this chain ultimately leads to Cathepsin E. Similarly, estradiol is also associated with Monoclonal antibody (mAb) 2E2, which targets 4-(2-Hydroxyethyl)-5-methyloxazole, and this target also leads to Cathepsin E. Additionally, estradiol is linked to mAb 2E2, which targets Notoamide D, which also leads to Cathepsin E. These findings indicate that estradiol may influence Cathepsin E through various molecular mechanisms, possibly involving signaling pathways or protein interactions that are not fully elucidated. The evidence is consistent with a complex network of interactions, where estradiol exerts its effects via different molecular mediators that ultimately converge on Cathepsin E. The strength of the evidence is moderate, with some pathways showing similar scores, suggesting that the relationship may be robust but not fully understood."
